class:: BlipDecay
summary:: Finite Blip with an exponential spectral envelope
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/BlipExtensions, Classes/BlipAD, Classes/BlipDiff, Classes/BlipDisp, Classes/BlipFB, Classes/BlipFrac, Classes/BlipInt, Classes/BlipKernel, Classes/BlipPM, Classes/BlipPeak, Classes/BlipPower, Classes/BlipSeries, Classes/BlipWidth

description::

BlipDecay weights harmonic code::k:: by code::decay ** (k - 1):: and divides
by the exact finite weight sum. Zero is a sinusoid, while one is an
equal-amplitude finite Blip.

A stable complex geometric-series evaluator handles phase zero and values very
close to one. code::decay:: is sample accurate; an unchanged scalar value
avoids modulation machinery outside the normal closed-form evaluation.
Harmonic topology changes fade over one block. Retained harmonics stop below
the Nyquist safety margin; this linear spectral weighting creates no new
sidebands.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/Blip::. BlipDecay equals it at one
but exposes a smooth spectral continuum down to a single sinusoid.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate; signed values are valid.

argument:: numharm
Requested harmonic count, sampled once per block and clipped to code::1..2048::
before Nyquist limiting.

argument:: decay
Audio- or control-rate exponential ratio, clipped to code::0..1::.

argument:: mul
Post-processing multiplier.

argument:: add
Post-processing offset.

examples::

code::
(
{ BlipDecay.ar(100, 60, 0.8, 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{ BlipDecay.ar(80, 80, SinOsc.ar(0.1).range(0, 1), 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
// Modulating all parameters for a dynamic sound
(
{ BlipDecay.ar(SinOsc.kr(0.1).exprange(50, 500), MouseX.kr(1, 100), MouseY.kr(0.01, 0.99)) ! 2 * 0.1 }.play;
)
::
